text:
sam: Heads-up — I'm starting the AWS KMS integration plan for Redwood Private (VPC) control plane + dataplane. Quick design Q: single CMK for both control-plane and data-plane or separate CMKs? Also need guidance on grants vs IAM roles for DEK caching. ping @anya @maria

anya: strong preference for separate CMKs. Control-plane CMK (Redwood-managed) for metadata, sign/verify, provisioning ops. Dataplane CMK should be customer-owned BYOK when requested. Envelope pattern: KEK = CMK, DEK = generated per-tenant/model, DEKs encrypted with KEK and cached locally. Keeping separation reduces blast radius and simplifies compliance.

maria: +1 separate keys. Also callout on audit logging: enable CloudTrail for KMS API calls (Encrypt, Decrypt, GenerateDataKey, ReEncrypt, CreateGrant, RetireGrant). Make sure encryptionContext is populated and logged — we'll use it to correlate request -> tenant. Add a CloudWatch metric filter for anomalous decrypt rate.

joseph: RBAC notes: control-plane service role needs kms:GenerateDataKey and kms:Encrypt for its CMK but should never have kms:Decrypt on customer DEKs. Dataplane instances need kms:Decrypt for tenant DEKs but scoped by encryptionContext and a condition on kms:ViaService or sourceVpc endpoint. Prefer using grants for short-lived decrypt access from dataplane workers (grants are auditable and revocable). Example policy snippet for dataplane role (abbreviated):

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:us-west-2:111122223333:key/xxxx",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "kms:EncryptionContext:tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

joseph: Also recommend enabling GrantTokens during GenerateDataKey so worker can immediately use cached DEK without race. Remember grants expire or can be retired.

engbot: deploy-bot created grant G-0a1b2c for dataplane-role in dev. (grant-id: G-0a1b2c)

sam: Rotation question — if CMK rotates or we perform a key replacement for BYOK, what's the safe rewrap path for all stored DEKs? Do we ReEncrypt through KMS or re-generate DEKs and re-encrypt payload? Thoughts on in-place vs bulk rewrap job?

joseph: For CMK rotation you can use KMS ReEncrypt to rewrap DEKs: call ReEncrypt with SourceKeyId = old KEK and DestinationKeyId = new KEK. That's atomic from KMS side and preserves ciphertext. For BYOK where Redwood can't call KMS, require customer to provide rotated key or perform a rewrap step in their environment. Plan: background rewrap job that streams metadata records, calls ReEncrypt (with pagination), and retries on throttling. Keep original CMK disabled until rewrap completes in case of compromise. Example CLI rewrap pattern: `aws kms re-encrypt --ciphertext-blob fileb://dek.enc --destination-key-id alias/new-keK`

anya: Add to that — update DEK cache invalidation after rewrap so workers fetch the new wrapped DEK. Also include a short TTL on cached plaintext DEKs and refresh on decrypt errors.

maria: Incident playbook snippet: if CMK compromise suspected -> 1) retire/disable CMK, 2) revoke grants and rotate keys, 3) trigger rewrap job for DEKs, 4) increase logging retention/forensics export to secure S3, 5) notify legal/compliance. Make sure CloudTrail logs are preserved (S3 bucket with MFA delete if required).

sam: TL;dr: separate CMKs, strict IAM + grants for dataplane, require encryptionContext checks, implement rewrap job + cache invalidation, and incident rotation playbook. I'll draft a runbook and schedule a security review next week. :thumbsup:
